::ALG1_U01_UA_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression 8x + 5, what is the coefficient? {
=A. 8#Correct. The coefficient is the number multiplying the variable x.
~B. x#This is the variable, not the coefficient. The coefficient is the numerical factor.
~C. 5#This is the constant term. It does not multiply the variable.
~D. 13#This is the sum of 8 and 5, not a part name from the expression.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>In the expression 6a + 9, what is the constant? {
~A. 6#This is the coefficient because it multiplies a.
=B. 9#Correct. The constant is the term with no variable.
~C. a#This is the variable, not the constant.
~D. 15#This is the sum of the visible numbers, not the constant term.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 3x + 4 when x = 5. {
~A. 12#This adds 3 and 4 first and does not substitute correctly.
~B. 15#This evaluates 3x but leaves out the +4.
=C. 19#Correct. Substitute 5 for x: 3(5) + 4 = 15 + 4 = 19.
~D. 35#This treats 3x as 35. Substitute and multiply before adding.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2(6 + 4) - 3. {
~A. 13#This adds inside the parentheses and subtracts 3, but it leaves out multiplication by 2.
=B. 17#Correct. 6 + 4 = 10, 2(10) = 20, and 20 - 3 = 17.
~C. 7#This adds 6 and 4, then subtracts 3, but leaves out multiplication by 2.
~D. 23#This adds 3 instead of subtracting it.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A class raises $45 on Monday and $30 on Tuesday. Which expression represents the total amount raised? {
~A. 45 - 30#This represents the difference, not the total.
~B. 45 &divide; 30#This represents a quotient, not the total amount.
=C. 45 + 30#Correct. A total is found by adding the amounts.
~D. 45 &middot; 30#This represents multiplication, not combining two amounts.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which value is equivalent to 0.25? {
~A. 2.5%#This moves the decimal one place too few for percent form.
=B. 25%#Correct. 0.25 equals 25%.
~C. 250%#This moves the decimal one place too many for percent form.
~D. 0.25%#This keeps the decimal but labels it as a percent, which changes the value.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A car travels 180 miles in 3 hours. What is the unit rate in miles per hour? {
~A. 30 miles per hour#This divides by 6 instead of by 3.
=B. 60 miles per hour#Correct. 180 &divide; 3 = 60.
~C. 90 miles per hour#This divides by 2 instead of by 3.
~D. 540 miles per hour#This multiplies 180 by 3 instead of dividing.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression represents 4 more than a number n? {
~A. 4n#This means 4 times a number, not 4 more than a number.
=B. n + 4#Correct. 4 more than n means add 4 to n.
~C. n - 4#This means 4 less than n.
~D. <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>n</sub>#This represents division, not addition.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair of terms are like terms? {
~A. 4x and 4#A variable term and a constant are not like terms.
=B. 3a and 8a#Correct. Both terms have the same variable part, a.
~C. 2x and 2x<sup>2</sup>#These are not like terms because the exponents are different.
~D. 5m and 5n#These are not like terms because the variables are different.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -7 + 12. {
~A. -19#This adds the absolute values and keeps the negative sign. The signs are different.
=B. 5#Correct. Moving 12 units right from -7 lands at 5.
~C. -5#This has the sign reversed. The positive value has greater absolute value.
~D. 19#This adds absolute values and ignores the negative sign.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate -4(3). {
~A. -1#This adds -4 and 3 instead of multiplying.
=B. -12#Correct. A negative times a positive is negative, and 4 &middot; 3 = 12.
~C. 12#This ignores the negative sign.
~D. 7#This adds the absolute values instead of multiplying.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which is equivalent to <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> as a decimal? {
~A. 0.34#This reads the fraction digits as a decimal instead of dividing.
~B. 0.43#This reverses the numerator and denominator.
=C. 0.75#Correct. 3 &divide; 4 = 0.75.
~D. 1.25#This is greater than 1, but <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub> is less than 1.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression shows the product of 6 and a number x? {
~A. 6 + x#This represents a sum, not a product.
=B. 6x#Correct. 6x means 6 multiplied by x.
~C. x - 6#This represents a difference.
~D. <sup>6</sup>&frasl;<sub>x</sub>#This represents a quotient.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 18 &divide; 3 + 2. {
~A. 3#This adds before dividing. Division comes before addition.
=B. 8#Correct. 18 &divide; 3 = 6, and 6 + 2 = 8.
~C. 5#This divides 18 by 3 + 2. There are no grouping symbols around 3 + 2.
~D. 12#This divides incorrectly or adds too early. Follow order of operations.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>A price increases from $40 to $50. What is the percent increase? {
~A. 10%#This uses the dollar change as the percent.
=B. 25%#Correct. The increase is 10, and 10 out of 40 is 25%.
~C. 40%#This uses the original price as the percent.
~D. 50%#This uses the new price as the percent.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is already simplified? {
~A. 3x + 5x#These are like terms and can be combined.
=B. 4x + 7#Correct. 4x and 7 are not like terms, so they cannot be combined.
~C. 2(a + 6)#This can be rewritten using the distributive property.
~D. 9m - 4m#These are like terms and can be combined.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2<sup>4</sup>. {
~A. 8#This multiplies 2 by 4. An exponent means repeated multiplication.
=B. 16#Correct. 2<sup>4</sup> = 2 &middot; 2 &middot; 2 &middot; 2 = 16.
~C. 6#This adds the base and exponent.
~D. 24#This multiplies too many factors of 2.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 5<sup>0</sup>. {
=A. 1#Correct. Any nonzero number raised to the zero power equals 1.
~B. 0#A zero exponent does not make the value zero.
~C. 5#This keeps the base unchanged, but the zero exponent property gives 1.
~D. 10#This adds 5 and 5 or doubles the base. Use the zero exponent property.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 3<sup>2</sup> &middot; 3<sup>1</sup>. {
~A. 9#This evaluates only 3<sup>2</sup> and ignores the extra factor.
~B. 18#This doubles 9 instead of multiplying by 3.
=C. 27#Correct. 3<sup>2</sup> &middot; 3<sup>1</sup> = 3<sup>3</sup> = 27.
~D. 81#This uses one exponent too many. The exponents add to 3.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 4<sup>3</sup> &divide; 4<sup>1</sup>. {
~A. 4#This stops at 4<sup>1</sup>. The quotient is 4<sup>2</sup>.
~B. 12#This subtracts or divides incorrectly. Use the quotient rule for powers.
~C. 64#This evaluates only 4<sup>3</sup> and ignores the division.
=D. 16#Correct. 4<sup>3</sup> &divide; 4<sup>1</sup> = 4<sup>2</sup> = 16.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 16<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup>. {
=A. 4#Correct. An exponent of <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub> means square root, and the square root of 16 is 4.
~B. 8#This divides 16 by 2. The exponent represents a root.
~C. 32#This multiplies 16 by 2 instead of finding the square root.
~D. 256#This squares 16 instead of finding the square root.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 27<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub></sup>. {
~A. 9#This divides 27 by 3. The exponent asks for a cube root.
=B. 3#Correct. 27<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub></sup> is the cube root of 27, which is 3.
~C. 81#This multiplies 27 by 3 instead of finding the cube root.
~D. 729#This cubes 9 or uses the wrong operation.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 10<sup>-2</sup>. {
~A. -100#A negative exponent does not make the value negative.
~B. 100#This ignores the negative exponent.
=C. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub>#Correct. 10<sup>-2</sup> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>10<sup>2</sup></sub> = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>100</sub>.
~D. -20#This multiplies 10 by -2 instead of using exponent meaning.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 25<sup><sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup>. {
~A. 15#This multiplies 25 by <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>. A rational exponent uses a root and a power.
~B. 75#This multiplies 25 by 3 instead of using the square root.
=C. 125#Correct. 25<sup><sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup> = (5)<sup>3</sup> = 125.
~D. 625#This squares 25 instead of using the exponent <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate (2<sup>3</sup>)<sup>2</sup>. {
~A. 12#This multiplies visible numbers incorrectly. Evaluate the power of a power.
~B. 32#This uses 2<sup>5</sup>. The exponents multiply, not add.
=C. 64#Correct. (2<sup>3</sup>)<sup>2</sup> = 2<sup>6</sup> = 64.
~D. 256#This uses too large an exponent.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q26::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q26<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 6<sup>2</sup> - 10. {
~A. 2#This subtracts before evaluating the exponent.
=B. 26#Correct. 6<sup>2</sup> = 36, and 36 - 10 = 26.
~C. 62#This reads 6<sup>2</sup> as 62 instead of 36.
~D. 12#This evaluates 6 &middot; 2 instead of 6<sup>2</sup>.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q27::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q27<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 81<sup><sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub></sup>. {
~A. 40.5#This divides 81 by 2. The exponent means square root.
=B. 9#Correct. The square root of 81 is 9.
~C. 162#This multiplies 81 by 2 instead of finding the square root.
~D. 6#This is not the square root of 81. Check which number squared equals 81.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q28::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q28<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.1<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 2<sup>5</sup> &divide; 2<sup>2</sup>. {
~A. 4#This subtracts too much from the exponent or evaluates incorrectly.
=B. 8#Correct. 2<sup>5</sup> &divide; 2<sup>2</sup> = 2<sup>3</sup> = 8.
~C. 16#This uses 2<sup>4</sup> instead of 2<sup>3</sup>.
~D. 128#This adds the exponents instead of subtracting them.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q29::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q29<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite x<sup>4</sup> &middot; x<sup>3</sup>. {
~A. x<sup>12</sup>#This multiplies exponents. For product of powers with the same base, add exponents.
=B. x<sup>7</sup>#Correct. x<sup>4</sup> &middot; x<sup>3</sup> = x<sup>4+3</sup> = x<sup>7</sup>.
~C. 2x<sup>7</sup>#This adds an extra coefficient. The expression only has powers of x.
~D. x#This subtracts exponents instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q30::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q30<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite (a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup>. {
~A. a<sup>5</sup>#This adds the exponents. A power raised to a power uses multiplication.
=B. a<sup>6</sup>#Correct. (a<sup>2</sup>)<sup>3</sup> = a<sup>2 &middot; 3</sup> = a<sup>6</sup>.
~C. 3a<sup>2</sup>#This treats the outside exponent as a coefficient.
~D. a<sup>8</sup>#This squares and then adds incorrectly. Multiply the exponents.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q31::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q31<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite y<sup>8</sup> &divide; y<sup>3</sup>. {
~A. y<sup>11</sup>#This adds the exponents. Division with the same base uses subtraction.
=B. y<sup>5</sup>#Correct. y<sup>8</sup> &divide; y<sup>3</sup> = y<sup>8-3</sup> = y<sup>5</sup>.
~C. y<sup>24</sup>#This multiplies the exponents instead of subtracting.
~D. y<sup>2</sup>#This divides the exponents instead of subtracting.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q32::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q32<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 4x + 6x. {
~A. 24x#This multiplies coefficients. Like terms are combined by adding coefficients.
=B. 10x#Correct. 4x + 6x = 10x.
~C. 10x<sup>2</sup>#This changes the variable part. Combining like terms keeps x.
~D. 4x + 6#This changes 6x into a constant.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q33::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q33<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 9m - 2m + 4. {
~A. 11m + 4#This adds 9m and 2m instead of subtracting.
=B. 7m + 4#Correct. 9m - 2m = 7m, and the constant 4 remains.
~C. 7m#This leaves out the constant 4.
~D. 11m#This adds the m-terms and leaves out the constant.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q34::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q34<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 3(x + 5) using the distributive property. {
~A. 3x + 5#This does not multiply 3 by 5.
=B. 3x + 15#Correct. Multiply 3 by x and by 5.
~C. 8x#This adds 3 and 5 and attaches x.
~D. 15x#This multiplies 3 and 5 but leaves out the x-term structure.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q35::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q35<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite 4(2n - 3) using the distributive property. {
~A. 8n - 3#This does not multiply 4 by -3.
~B. 6n - 12#This adds 4 and 2n instead of multiplying.
=C. 8n - 12#Correct. 4 times 2n is 8n, and 4 times -3 is -12.
~D. 8n + 12#This changes the subtraction to addition.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q36::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q36<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 2(x + 4) + 3x. {
~A. 2x + 8#This distributes correctly but does not combine the additional 3x.
~B. 5x + 4#This does not multiply 2 by 4.
=C. 5x + 8#Correct. 2(x + 4) = 2x + 8, and 2x + 3x = 5x.
~D. 8x#This combines the constant with variable terms.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q37::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q37<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to 5a + 3a? {
~A. 15a#This multiplies the coefficients. Like terms are added.
=B. 8a#Correct. 5a + 3a = 8a.
~C. 8a<sup>2</sup>#This changes the variable part. Combining like terms keeps a.
~D. 2a#This subtracts the coefficients instead of adding.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q38::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q38<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression is equivalent to 2(3x + 1) + x? {
~A. 6x + 1#This does not multiply 2 by 1 and does not combine the extra x.
~B. 6x + 2#This distributes correctly but does not combine the extra x.
=C. 7x + 2#Correct. 2(3x + 1) = 6x + 2, and 6x + x = 7x.
~D. 8x + 2#This treats x as 2x. The coefficient of x is 1.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q39::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q39<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>A student wrote 6b + 2b = 8b<sup>2</sup>. What is the correct simplification? {
~A. 12b#This multiplies the coefficients. Like terms are combined by adding.
=B. 8b#Correct. Add the coefficients and keep the variable part b.
~C. 8b<sup>2</sup>#This repeats the mistake. Adding like terms does not square the variable.
~D. 4b#This subtracts the coefficients instead of adding them.
}

::ALG1_U01_UA_Q40::
Question ID:<br>ALG1_U01_UA_Q40<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A1.FND.2<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify 5(q - 2) + q. {
~A. 5q - 2#This does not multiply 5 by -2 and does not combine the extra q.
~B. 6q + 10#This uses the wrong sign for the constant.
=C. 6q - 10#Correct. 5(q - 2) = 5q - 10, and 5q + q = 6q.
~D. 4q - 10#This subtracts q instead of adding q.
}
